Z||000001||jmw||08-14-2000||07:38 PM||||That's very generous of you. I'm actually not sure what to get. A Haynes is $13+ tax. Chilton's is $15 + tax. Problem is, some people recommend one and not the other. Some people recommend both, and then some recommend neither -- they say just get a P manual and be done with it. Which reminds me - are a P manual and a Hames manual the same thing? [I know Haynes and Hames are different]. Also, what is a Clymer manual? I don't want to blow all my part money on books, capiche?<P>Would appreciate your opinion. Since all the manuals are Greek to me at this point, it's very important that I get whatever's "easiest" to understand - if there is such a thing. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-15-2000||01:52 AM||||The P22 is a dealer/shop manual showing all of the exploded illustrations and part #s.  The Haynes and Clymers manuals are rather generic IMO.  There is also a CD-ROM by Popular Mechanics.  If you're serious about working on your own car, I would suggest one of each.  None of them has all of the complete info you need.  I've never heard of a Hames manual.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||08-15-2000||08:20 AM||||I believe the Helms manual for the specific year model is by far the best (although it still lacks a few details).||||reg||1
Z||000004||nivlek68||08-15-2000||08:52 AM||||i got the p.m. cd for a 86 sc,it is aright,you still need one of the manuals though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||08-15-2000||05:52 PM||||Helms manuals are *the* factory manual and contain far more detail than many people want. Helms is just the publishing and distribution company subcontacted by GM.<P>It's rare for the Clymers/Haynes etc to have more detail. ||||reg||3
